WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:12.310
So what we'll do today is finish
up the discussion of barrier

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.250
methods, maybe start on these L1
convex cardinality type thing.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.080
So let's take a look at that.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.820
So just to remind
you what it was,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:26.620
we are going to look at
using a barrier method.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.230
Barrier methods are a
pretty simple thing.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.970
Let me go find it.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:31.730
There we go.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.510
So it simply says we find a
point on the central path.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.330
You use Newton's
method to do that.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.420
Then you increment this
barrier parameter T

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.590
that controls the gap.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.420
And you compute the new point.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.310
And you keep doing this.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.890
And at some point,
actually, every time

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:56.140
you center, you end up with
two things, strictly feasible

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.190
primal point--

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.270
that's x.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.960
You also end up with
strictly feasible dual point.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.760
And you have a gap, that means.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.500
And the gap is
precisely m over t.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.210
t is that parameter
that controls

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.520
the level of approximation.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.730
So basically, you
keep increasing t

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.400
until m over t,
which is the gap,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.490
is less than whatever
your threshold-- whatever

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:20.960
your desired threshold is.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.070
So that's the method.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.720
I think we talked
about that last time.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.580
This is a homotopy method.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.280
It's kind of a special one
because general homotopy

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.720
methods are much more complex
because paths can just stop

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.880
or they can bifurcate,
or they can just

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.290
start in the middle of nowhere.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.520
Here, that's false.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.310
And no matter where
you are, you can always

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.690
get back to the central path.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:43.550
It may cost you a
bunch of Newton steps,

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.960
but you can always do it.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.730
OK, that's the barrier method.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.990
And what we're going
to do now is look at--

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.870
we're going to look at the
complexity analysis of it.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.270
I think I mentioned
last time that according

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:00.410
to the classical
analysis, the upper bound

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.620
on the number of Newton
steps grows as the barrier

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.810
method-- as t increases.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:10.070
And so if you believed that
upper bound had any meaning,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:13.250
you might actually shy away
from even attempting this

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.170
because you'd say,
well, sure, as t

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.507
gets bigger and
bigger, the problems

00:02:17.507 --> 00:02:19.590
we solve by Newton's method
get harder and harder,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.470
it takes more and more
iterations, and so on and so

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:22.080
forth.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.520
And then you get into
some kind of doom spiral

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.540
there or whatever.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.930
This actually kind of
happened in the '60s, weirdly.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:33.080
I don't know why.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.537
So it turns out that's not true.

00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:36.620
You could have guessed
that because you've already

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.380
seen empirical results for how
well the barrier method works,

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.330
which is, by the way,
just unbelievably well.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.350
So basically-- and it's got
a very weird interpretation.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.030
The one interpretation
of its working

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.120
so well is that it
takes between 20--

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.960
you solve between 20 and
50 least squares problems

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.883
to solve a convex
optimization problem

00:02:57.883 --> 00:03:00.050
with inequality constraints,
all that kind of stuff.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:01.430
It's crazy.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:03.050
Why do I say you
solve least squares

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:06.950
problems is because you do
between 20 and 50 Newtons

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.270
steps total.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:13.775
But a Newton step is actually
solving a set of kkt--

00:03:13.775 --> 00:03:16.670
a set of linear kkt
systems, which is precisely

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:18.830
solving a least squares--
it's exactly the same

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.420
as solving a least
squares problem

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.350
with equality constraints.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.890
So it's actually kind of cool.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:31.700
So you can-- says basically you
can solve any convex problem

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.690
by solving between 20 and 50
least squares problems that

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.790
have the same
structure and so on.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.170
So that's actually kind of--

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.120
I mean, I find that
kind of interesting.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.650
And by the way, also--

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.970
well, let's see.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.300
If people had been
awake in the '60s,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.550
people would have noticed this.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.770
So this wasn't mainstream until
the '90s or something like.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.090
But before then, this would
have been just completely

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.920
radical and unheard of.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.402
OK, that's the background.

00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:01.860
Now what we're
going to do is let's

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.318
see if we can get the theory
to catch up with the practice.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.230
And that comes via
self-concordance analysis.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:11.820
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.950
going to simply assume
that the problems--

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.607
that the centering problem, the
objective, is self-concordant.

00:04:17.607 --> 00:04:19.440
And that's true for,
actually, a whole bunch

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.950
of the things that we actually
care about, like logs.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.500
And then this is the entire--
this is basically all of it.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.030
This is also the densest chain
of inequalities, I think,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.392
that have appeared in lecture
slides that I'm aware of.

00:04:33.392 --> 00:04:35.100
I couldn't follow it,
so I had to work it

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.280
out myself this morning.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.960
So we will go through
this together now a bit.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.990
I mean, eventually
I followed it.

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.790
So let's remember
what we're doing.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:48.910
We're on the central
path at a point--

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:50.210
I'll draw a central path.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.120
So here's the central path.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.500
Here's the optimal point.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.990
Here we are.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:05:01.300
So we're at x of t.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.910
And then we're going
to increase t by mu.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:05.650
We're going to have
t times equals mu.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.620
And this is going
to be x of mu t.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.950
And we're going to call
this guy x plus, like that.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:16.540
So we're going to do a step
of the barrier method, which

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.050
is to use Newton's method
to go from here to here.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.010
So that's what we're
going to analyze.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:25.780
What we're going to
try to do is bound

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.700
the number of steps
it takes to do

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.270
that-- number of Newton steps.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:35.200
OK, so we go back to self
concordance analysis,

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.710
and we ask, well,
how many Newton steps

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:43.840
does it take to compute this
thing starting from here?

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.170
And the answer is you look at
the function you're minimizing,

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.400
which is--

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:49.090
it's this.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:50.980
It's mu t.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.828
So we start when
you-- let's see.

00:05:53.828 --> 00:05:55.120
Let me write down what this is.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:03.460
So x plus is the
argmin, right, of t mu,

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:12.250
or I'll write it as mu t, f0 of
x plus sum and this is over i

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:17.960
of this is the barriers, right?

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:18.460
Right.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.760
And then plus the
indicator function.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.550
I guess we write
it as Ax equals b.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:28.610
Looks like that.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:29.110
OK?

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:34.240
So you're basically minimizing
this function subject

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.190
to these equality
constraints, right?

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.050
So that's literally
the definition

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.580
of what x plus is, right?

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.040
If I remove this, this
is the definition of x.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.900
And, in fact, that's
the point, right?

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:48.500
The whole point is we
just computed this.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.780
Now we increase
t and so the idea

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:53.150
is this problem is very--
should be relatively

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:54.980
close to the
previous one and that

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.750
hopefully will save
us some Newton steps

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:57.770
or something like that.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:58.890
That's the hope here.

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.350
So this is the minimizer.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.325
OK.

00:07:01.325 --> 00:07:03.890
So the general
self-concordance theory

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:07.790
says that the number of steps,
the number of Newton steps,

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.760
is less than or equal
to the function value

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.000
where you start minus the
function value where you end,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.240
which is its optimal value, and
then multiplied by a constant.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.250
And you could either
use our lazy constant

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:22.170
is like you divide by 365
or something like that.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.720
Or maybe you multiply by 360--

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.580
I think you multiply by 365.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:28.250
And then you add something
that looks like log, log,

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:31.460
1 over epsilon, which we just
call 5 or 6 and that's fine.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:33.110
Everybody got that?

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:36.410
So that's the c
over there, right?

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:42.620
So this is the function value
at x of t, which we're just

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.090
going to call x over here.

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:45.870
That's this guy here.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:50.930
And then this is going to be the
function value when you finish.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:56.180
Because x plus minimizes this
thing subject to the equality

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:56.990
constraints, right?

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.250
So that's what that is.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:58.890
OK.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.700
So that's the number
of Newton iterations.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:06.740
And our job is to get an upper
bound on this big thing here.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.798
Forget the c for
the moment and let's

00:08:09.798 --> 00:08:11.090
just take a look at this thing.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.870
And now we go very
slowly or I'll

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:17.840
expand various things, right?

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.240
So the first question
is how on Earth

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.860
do you get-- how do you go
from this line to this line?

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.440
And that's an
equality constraint.

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:28.850
And so I think that
is written here.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:33.320
We use the fact that phi
of x minus phi of x plus.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.230
So phi of x is the sum of the--

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:41.540
it's minus the sum of the
log of minus fi of x, right?

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:45.170
This is the same thing
evaluated at x plus.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.360
So this difference
looks like that.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:48.620
It doesn't look like it.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:49.760
It's equal to it, right?

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.520
So this is the
difference, right?

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:53.540
It's this rate.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.010
The log of this ratio.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:56.683
I'll leave the minus
signs there just--

00:08:56.683 --> 00:08:58.850
I don't know-- of course,
you could get rid of them.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:08:59.930
They're both negative.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.330
Both fi of x and fi of
x plus because they're

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.070
both strictly feasible.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.180
OK.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:08.300
Now, now we're going
to use the following.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.990
Lambda i, these are
the dual variables

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.780
associated-- the inequality
constraints associated

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:16.880
with computing a point
on the central path.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.140
That's equal to this.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:21.410
And you realize that, hey,
this looks like that, right?

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:25.355
So it says minus 1 over fi
or 1 over minus fi of x--

00:09:25.355 --> 00:09:27.320
that's the denominator here--

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:28.970
is equal to t lambda i.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:33.650
So I plug t lambda i in instead
of that and I get this, OK?

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.965
So that I think--

00:09:36.965 --> 00:09:39.290
that makes sense.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:39.830
OK.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.370
And I do that.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:44.060
And then what I'm going
to do very weirdly

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.290
is I'm going to
do the following.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:50.450
If you don't mind, I'm going
to put a mu here and then

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.560
go minus--

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.210
let's see, minus--

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:53.960
I'm going to put--

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.580
I'm going to put a mu in there.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.430
And then I'm going to
say maybe plus log mu.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:00.530
I think I got that right, right?

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:05.960
Because here, I multiply
this thing by mu.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.150
There's a minus sign and I
think I'd go plus log mu.

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:08.840
I think that's right.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.740
Is that right or did I
do that the wrong way?

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:11.510
I think I did it--

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:13.460
I did it the wrong way.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.755
Let me see.

00:10:15.755 --> 00:10:17.240
So it looked like that.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.590
I'm going to
multiply this by mu.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.330
So I have to do this.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.140
There we go.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.535
And now that didn't change.

00:10:24.535 --> 00:10:27.390
So this is going to be
what you see over there.

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:31.620
And this is a sum over i of log
mu, but there's m in that sum.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.425
So that's m log mu.

00:10:33.425 --> 00:10:36.950
So I think we got to--

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:38.885
we've verified this line here.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:42.280
So OK.

00:10:44.805 --> 00:10:47.275
Next up, we're going to go
from this line to this line.

00:10:47.275 --> 00:10:48.900
A lot of the terms
are the same, right?

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:50.170
These are the same.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.280
That's the same.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:56.820
And here, I'm only going to
use one inequality, which,

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.320
again, goes back to
week two of the class.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:00.690
Which is just--

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:02.220
I'm going to use
Jensen's inequality

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.290
that says that for any
positive number log

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:08.010
a is less than a minus 1, right?

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.508
By the way, all these weird
inequalities that you know,

00:11:11.508 --> 00:11:13.770
now you'll probably
remember that--

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:16.187
you'll probably recognize
that almost all of them

00:11:16.187 --> 00:11:18.270
follow directly-- they're
just Jensen's inequality

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.145
for something, right?

00:11:19.145 --> 00:11:20.160
So this just says--

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.090
this just says look at the
Taylor expansion of log

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:29.310
a around a equals 1 and you
get this inequality, OK?

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:35.265
So that means here, I
can take this thing.

00:11:35.265 --> 00:11:38.430
If I replace-- if I remove
the log and subtract 1,

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:39.520
I get a lower bound.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:40.900
So that's this next term here.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.270
So I get this.

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:43.300
I get here.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:44.380
I get minus 1.

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:46.390
I sum over m of them
so I get minus m

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:48.790
and I get this thing because
that's just that down here.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:51.850
And I pulled out the mu and
the t that don't depend on i.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:56.260
So OK.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.596
Now the next one is this.

00:11:59.596 --> 00:12:03.970
The next trick is to recognize
a couple of things here.

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:05.335
OK, here it is.

00:12:05.335 --> 00:12:08.290
We recognize mu t.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.160
Forget the mu t on
these two terms.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:16.990
This is f0 of x plus and this
is some lambda i, fi of x plus.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:19.840
But that basically
is nothing but--

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:24.730
that is exactly the Lagrangian--
let me write that out.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:28.900
That's actually
the Lagrangian at--

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:33.290
let's see that's lambda
nu and x plus, right?

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:36.820
That's what that is, right?

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:39.430
Because that's the-- Oh,
there's one more term.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:43.150
The other term would be to
have a nu transpose Ax minus b,

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:44.035
but x is feasible.

00:12:44.035 --> 00:12:47.740
So Ax is equal b and that other
term, it doesn't appear there.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:56.180
So l of lambda nu x plus is
precisely f0 plus sum lambda i,

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:58.520
fi, OK?

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:01.400
But we know the following.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.040
This thing is for
sure bigger than

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.190
or equal to g of lambda nu.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.790
Because the dual
function is what

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:15.280
minimizes this over all x's and
this is just a particular x.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.600
So this is for sure true.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.740
So then what we do, we go
back over here in the world's

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.440
densest one slide derivation.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:28.250
And we replace the f0
of x plus some lambda i,

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:32.210
fi with a lower bound,
which is the g, OK?

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:33.217
And that goes to right--

00:13:33.217 --> 00:13:34.550
I think that goes the right way.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:37.880
It does go the right way, right?

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.290
OK.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.290
Now we look at this thing.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:45.050
And we say, well, wait a minute.

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:52.250
f0 of x minus g of lambda nu
is the gap at x lambda nu.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:54.965
But that's precisely m over t.

00:13:54.965 --> 00:13:57.965
That's the calculation
we did before, right?

00:13:57.965 --> 00:14:01.790
So this minus g,
that's m over t.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:05.880
So I have mu t times m over t
and I end up with just this.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:10.355
So I think that was coherent.

00:14:10.355 --> 00:14:13.220
So OK.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:14.150
Yeah?

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:18.080
Isn't that the i is associated
with x instead of x plus?

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.100
Which one?

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:19.790
Lambda i.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:21.060
Is that, define lambda i--

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:21.560
No, no.

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:22.850
The lambda i's are--

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:25.760
the lambda i's are
with respect to x here.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:28.930
Yeah, not x plus.

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:34.000
But Langrangian tell you--

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:38.330
equate to g is, in terms
of x plus, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:38.830
No.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:39.130
No, no.

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:39.800
Certainly not.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:41.870
It is up here.

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.000
This is where--

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.330
To get this, I replace the x
plus with x in the Lagrangian.

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:47.844
So no.

00:14:47.844 --> 00:14:50.450
This is right.

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:51.690
Yeah?

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:53.370
OK.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:54.030
All right.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:57.340
So what does mu minus 1
minus log mu look like?

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:01.530
So let me draw that.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.160
So that's going to look
something like this, right?

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:09.960
So it's mu minus 1 minus log mu.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.960
Now mu is bigger than 1, right?

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:15.320
Because mu is the amount
by which we crank up

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.910
t at each step of
the barrier method.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:18.800
So this is 1.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.780
This thing is 0 here.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:23.600
Also, its derivative is 0
here and its second derivative

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.370
is 1, right?

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:30.060
So this looks like at least
locally something like that.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:30.930
A square.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.595
That's what that looks like.

00:15:32.595 --> 00:15:34.020
And what this says--

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.180
I mean, you end up with
a stunning answer, right?

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:39.270
It basically says
if I divide that,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.340
this multiplied by a
number and then you

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:44.700
add 6, that's an upper bound
on the number of Newton steps

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:53.550
it takes you to compute the x
of mu t starting from x of t.

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.830
These are two points on
the central path, right?

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:56.760
And that's the bound.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:58.200
And it's actually cool.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:02.670
What it does is it does say that
as mu gets bigger and bigger,

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.110
the bound goes up.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.572
But it does say that if you
take like really small steps,

00:16:07.572 --> 00:16:09.030
it's not going to
take much at all.

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:10.830
Actually, at that
point, the 5 or the 6,

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.860
which is our way to say
log, log, 1 over epsilon,

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:15.510
is going to dominate, right?

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.860
So OK.

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:20.045
OK.

00:16:20.045 --> 00:16:22.130
Now you simply assemble things.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.180
You say, well, the number of
Newton's steps, remember, is--

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:28.430
this is exactly the
number of outer steps

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.830
you'll take because you'll
start with an initial gap.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:32.720
This is basically
the initial gap.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:35.540
And then divided by log mu.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:38.522
So if mu is really small, you'll
take a whole bunch of steps.

00:16:38.522 --> 00:16:39.980
But you'll make
small-- you'll make

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:41.813
a progress, which is
exactly a factor of mu,

00:16:41.813 --> 00:16:44.920
and duality gap each step.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:47.070
And then this is the
number of-- that's

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.290
our bound on the number
of-- the upper bound

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:54.010
on the number of Newton
steps to compute x at mu

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:57.730
t given you've just
computed it at xt, right?

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.765
And so now when you multiply
these two things together,

00:17:00.765 --> 00:17:02.390
you get something
that looks like this.

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:03.880
It's actually really cool.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:06.940
And here, I'm using our
lazy numbers, right?

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:10.270
Our lazy numbers in the bound,
I forget what they were.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:14.020
I think it's 365 times the
difference in objective value

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:15.670
plus 6 or something.

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:18.040
And you get something that
looks like this, right?

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.050
And this is very, very cool.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:23.079
What it does is it says there
is indeed a trade off of mu.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:26.380
If you take mu too small,
then you're going to be--

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:29.147
this term is going to
give you a lot of--

00:17:29.147 --> 00:17:30.730
you're going to have
to-- you're going

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:33.370
to have to do many, many
outer iterations, right?

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:36.190
If you take mu too
big, it says this term

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:38.920
is going to start dominating
and it'll look like that.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:41.950
And this says that somewhere,
for this particular problem

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:46.090
with 100 inequalities and a
duality gap reduction of 10

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:50.500
to the 5, it says that the
optimal mu is around 1.03

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:52.580
or something, right?

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:55.850
Actually, I'm pretty sure this
is using our lazy bound which

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:57.500
has got 365.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.900
A more careful Russian
bound, you know,

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:01.940
where you go through
lots of pages of analysis

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.220
makes that number
closer to 11 or 12.

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:04.970
So it's about 30.

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:07.220
And then I think actually
you get something, you know.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:10.820
It's actually
instead of 1.02, it's

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:13.083
something like 1.3, which
is at least less ridiculous.

00:18:13.083 --> 00:18:14.000
But it doesn't matter.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.260
It looks the same, right?

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:17.760
Right.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.010
So oh, and also you can
notice the number of--

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:22.500
you can look at the theory
here and it will say--

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.440
it will say the good news
is that the total number

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:31.110
of iterations is
less than 8,000.

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:32.590
There, there you go.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:33.090
OK.

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:36.700
So But we all know that
as an empirical fact.

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:39.300
It takes between 20
and 80 steps always.

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.060
There's never been any
experience otherwise.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.080
Everybody got this?

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.150
So I guess it's not so bad.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:46.835
If you take 8,000
and divide it by 36,

00:18:46.835 --> 00:18:49.650
which is the ratio of the
lazy bound to the other one,

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:53.205
you get something that's
much more reasonable I think.

00:18:53.205 --> 00:18:56.943
But anyway, OK.

00:18:56.943 --> 00:18:57.860
So that's pretty cool.

00:18:57.860 --> 00:18:59.600
So actually, I mean,
these two slides

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:02.390
would have been completely
radical in the year,

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:07.010
I don't know, 1990 or
something like that.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:09.650
And in fact, something
not too dissimilar

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:12.020
ended up on the cover
of The New York--

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:14.840
the front page of The New York
Times, which is another story,

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:16.740
but it's a weird one.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:17.240
OK.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:20.250
So OK.

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:23.490
Now if you want-- if you want
to go all the way on this, what

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:26.360
you do is you say, well, I
can take this function here,

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:28.860
which is my upper bound on the
total number of Newton steps.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.685
And I can minimize it
over mu, which you can do.

00:19:31.685 --> 00:19:33.060
I mean, it's not
that hard to do.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:34.980
You don't do it exactly
because all of this is sloppy.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:37.020
And it's all silly because
this is all upper bounds

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:38.145
and all that kind of stuff.

00:19:38.145 --> 00:19:39.630
But if you do, what
you'll find out

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.640
is that you would
like to choose mu

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:44.100
to be on the order
something like that.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:47.190
That's very close in order to
what the minimizer would be.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.500
If you plug that back in,
you get something amazing.

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:52.470
It says that the
number of steps is

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:55.140
it's on the order of
the square root of m

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.240
and then there's a log m term.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:58.110
I ignore that.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.830
I guess if you're a complexity
theorist, you don't, right?

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:02.610
The log m.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:05.340
So it's square root m,
log m is the complexity.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:09.840
I think people just quote
it as square root m, right?

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.605
So it's pretty good.

00:20:13.605 --> 00:20:16.710
So I mean, in fact, most
people would choose--

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:19.420
choose mu's that
are much larger.

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:24.240
And in fact, people refer to
methods that have small mu.

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:26.910
They refer to those
as short step methods.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:29.280
And then ones where
you actually are

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:32.580
much more aggressive
in adapting mu

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.050
are called long step methods.

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:36.592
You might see this if you start
looking at some of the papers

00:20:36.592 --> 00:20:39.210
from the '90s and the '00s
and all that kind of stuff.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.210
You would actually see
this in titles of papers

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:45.000
like a long step primal dual
method, blah, blah, blah.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:45.970
Something like that.

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:46.470
OK.

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:50.930
So this is actually like--

00:20:50.930 --> 00:20:52.820
I think it's pretty cool.

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.475
I mean this is--

00:20:55.475 --> 00:21:00.215
This is how the theory
and the practice

00:21:00.215 --> 00:21:03.005
are approximately consistent.

00:21:03.005 --> 00:21:05.240
But what I should tell you--

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:06.680
I mean, I'll
explain this later--

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:09.800
what I should tell you,
though, is that there's

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:13.790
been a leapfrog thing
where people working

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:16.790
on practical implementations
of interior point methods

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:20.210
or barrier methods actually
will do something not justified

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:21.880
by the theory.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:22.880
And it will work better.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:25.400
And they will run it on test
suites of thousands of problems

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:27.275
and blah, blah, blah,
release something maybe

00:21:27.275 --> 00:21:28.400
commercially or whatever.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:32.240
And what will happen then
typically is five years later,

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:35.150
the theorists will catch up and
show that a very-- a simplified

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:38.285
variation of that
method actually

00:21:38.285 --> 00:21:40.108
is like polynomial
time or something.

00:21:40.108 --> 00:21:42.650
And then, by then, the people
actually working on the methods

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:44.330
will come up with
something else, right?

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:45.150
And extend it.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:47.180
So there are
periods every couple

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.420
of years when the theory and
the practice are in sync,

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:53.810
but it doesn't last very
long as soon as that happens.

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:56.120
So that's actually
the story, but OK.

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:03.320
Well, first of all, any
questions about this?

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:04.560
It's cool stuff.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:09.605
But if not, we'll move on.

00:22:09.605 --> 00:22:13.220
You probably guessed that the
way everything was set up,

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:14.900
the same-- the
exact same methods

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:16.980
are going to work for
generalized inequalities,

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:17.480
right?

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:18.890
And so-- and you're right.

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:22.740
And this is how you solve like
SOCPs, semidefinite programs,

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:26.300
things like that, of
which you've solved

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:29.540
a whole bunch already so.

00:22:29.540 --> 00:22:30.955
Sorry, you didn't.

00:22:30.955 --> 00:22:35.330
But CVXPY-- You typed in CVXPY,
CVXPY compiled your problem

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:37.520
to some standard
form and then called

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:40.325
one of some number of solvers.

00:22:40.325 --> 00:22:45.545
So you have solved a bunch
of SDPs in that spirit.

00:22:45.545 --> 00:22:46.423
So OK.

00:22:46.423 --> 00:22:47.840
So here, what we're
going to do is

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:50.840
we're going to look at these--

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:54.200
we're going to have cone
constraints like this, right?

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:55.670
So fi is a vector.

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:58.110
And the most
interesting one is going

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:01.770
to be things like
SOCP and SDPs, right?

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:07.290
So if you think
about it, we have

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:10.575
to just go back and
generalize everything.

00:23:10.575 --> 00:23:15.300
Now before, we had fi of x.
fi of x was a number, right?

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.850
Minus fi of x we
thought as a margin.

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:21.480
And minus log minus fi of x
was this barrier function.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:22.170
It was convex.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:26.760
And as you got to the boundary,
the boundary being fi equals 0,

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.420
this thing would go
to plus infinity.

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:29.760
So that was our barrier.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:33.790
But now, I mean, let's
suppose I have some cone.

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:36.120
Let's imagine it's like
the semidefinite cone

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:37.230
or something like that.

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:39.240
You need to have
something that's going

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.920
to be the same as a logarithm.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:45.960
So you need the idea of
a logarithm on a cone.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:49.410
So what you do is
you generalize this

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:52.560
to have-- you say that
a generalized logarithm,

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.530
it's a function that is--
its domain is the interior.

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:57.940
So that makes perfect sense.

00:23:57.940 --> 00:23:59.040
It's concave.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:00.400
It's strictly concave.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:02.353
That's what this
second term mean--

00:24:02.353 --> 00:24:03.160
means.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:08.595
And in fact, this is
actually concave in the--

00:24:08.595 --> 00:24:11.130
let's just say it's
concave, right?

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:14.400
And then it has to actually
agree with a logarithm

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:16.170
on any ray, right?

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:18.040
And so it's going
to look like this.

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:21.750
If you multiply phi
with some scalar s here,

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:25.200
phi of sy, that's going
to be 5y plus theta log s.

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:29.640
So it has to look exactly
like a log on any ray.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:30.810
OK.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:32.590
So you do get things like this.

00:24:32.590 --> 00:24:36.180
Oh, and this number,
theta, is called the degree

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:37.950
of that logarithm, right?

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.755
So here, if you have
the nonnegative orthant,

00:24:40.755 --> 00:24:42.690
if you use this
constraint-- you just

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:45.750
end up with the same barrier
we had before, right,

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:51.240
or this is the log of
the nonnegative orthant.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:53.730
The log of a vector
is this thing, right?

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:56.460
Something like it's a
generalized logarithm.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.260
What's interesting is
much more interesting

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:01.260
is what happens on like
a semidefinite cone.

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:03.120
There, surprise,
surprise, our friend

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:06.100
log determinant, which
goes back, whatever,

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:08.650
to week two of the class.

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:11.470
Not surprisingly, that's
a generalized logarithm

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:13.620
on the positive definite cone.

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:14.120
OK.

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:16.700
So it's got a lot of the things
you would like on it, right?

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:17.770
It's also correct, right?

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.380
If you go an array of
matrices, we already saw that.

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:22.090
That's actually how
we proved concavity.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:24.610
Now this thing looks exactly
like a logarithm, right?

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:25.570
So that's it.

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:31.760
And here the so-called
degree is n, right?

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.480
And for a second
order cone, you get

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:36.350
something that looks like this.

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:39.790
This is not-- it's not totally
obvious that that's concave,

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:41.992
but it is.

00:25:41.992 --> 00:25:43.950
Actually, I don't think
it's that hard to show.

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:46.245
The only one that would be
tricky would be this part.

00:25:46.245 --> 00:25:47.340
So OK.

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:48.690
Sorry the others.

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:50.030
All right.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:51.405
So there's a bunch
of properties.

00:25:51.405 --> 00:25:53.740
If you have a
generalized logarithm,

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:56.680
you can figure out
what happens, right?

00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:02.770
One is that the gradient is
nonnegative in the dual cone.

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:07.420
If you have y transpose times
the gradient of the logarithm,

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:08.290
you just get theta.

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:10.760
It's just a constant.

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:13.210
And so for a
nonnegative orthant,

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:17.470
if you want to check that the
gradient is going to be this,

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:19.480
I guess you've seen that, right?

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:23.350
And then if you take y
transpose times this gradient,

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:24.040
you just get n.

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:26.830
Because you just-- you take y
times 1 over y and so on. y1

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:28.190
times 1 over y1 and so on.

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:29.380
So you just get this.

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:29.920
OK.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:32.007
And for positive
semidefinite cone,

00:26:32.007 --> 00:26:33.340
it's actually super interesting.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:35.920
The gradient of
the log determinant

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:38.230
is the inverse matrix, right?

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:40.345
You have to interpret
that super carefully,

00:26:40.345 --> 00:26:41.320
but that's what it is.

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:42.385
That's the inverse.

00:26:42.385 --> 00:26:45.160
And the inner product between
two symmetric matrices

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:46.660
is the trace of
the product, right?

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:48.490
Because that's actually
the inner product.

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:51.450
It's the sum of the products
of the corresponding entries,

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:51.950
right?

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:55.490
And so that, of course, is
n because trace of y times y

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:59.130
inverse is the trace
of i, which is n, OK?

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:00.860
So these things work.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:05.340
Second order cone, same story
if you work out what this is.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:05.840
OK.

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:07.310
So all right.

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:12.200
So that's your-- that's this
property of the logarithmic--

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:15.300
of the generalized logarithm.

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:18.390
Armed with that, we can actually
define a logarithmic barrier.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:20.520
So the log barrier
is going to be this.

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:23.140
I have a bunch of cone
inequalities, but they are--

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:23.640
Sorry.

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:25.432
I have a bunch of
inequalities, but they're

00:27:25.432 --> 00:27:26.610
respect to some cone here.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:30.750
Like here K1 and up
to K sub m, right?

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:32.322
So we form this thing.

00:27:32.322 --> 00:27:33.780
That's going to be
the log barrier.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.480
And this is exactly
like in the case

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:38.400
when the inequalities
were scalars.

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:39.990
It's identical, right?

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:42.792
Or at least the form
is identical, right?

00:27:42.792 --> 00:27:44.250
It's a bit more
complicated, right?

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:47.845
These are vectors now.

00:27:47.845 --> 00:27:49.390
And this thing is convex.

00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:51.580
It's twice continuously
differentiable.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.800
And this allows us to
talk about a central path.

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:56.050
But now we can talk about
things like the central path

00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:58.570
for a semidefinite program or
a second-order cone program.

00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:01.990
And it's actually
identical, right?

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:04.300
x star of t is the
minimum of this thing.

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:06.280
And the only difference
is we have changed--

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:11.800
we've generalized phi to
handle generalized inequalities

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:12.950
for the constraints.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:14.450
So the barrier has just changed.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:17.080
That's the only thing
that ever changed.

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:18.500
Makes sense?

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:21.803
So OK.

00:28:21.803 --> 00:28:23.470
Everything else is
going to work, right?

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:26.350
If you look at the
optimal-- if you minimize

00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:30.685
that the function t times
the function plus a barrier,

00:28:30.685 --> 00:28:32.963
then you will get this
thing called x star of t.

00:28:32.963 --> 00:28:34.630
That's the central
path-- that's a point

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:38.215
on the central path associated
with the parameter value t.

00:28:38.215 --> 00:28:40.210
And if you work out the
optimality condition,

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:41.810
it just looks like this.

00:28:41.810 --> 00:28:45.190
And when you work this
out, it's identical.

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:49.640
It says that if this holds,
then it turns out x star of t

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:52.550
also minimizes the Lagrangian
with this particular choice

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:54.980
of lambda i of t and nu of t.

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:58.190
And if you look back at what
it was for the scalar case,

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:00.445
it's just exactly the same.

00:29:00.445 --> 00:29:03.190
It's the same.

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:04.765
OK.

00:29:04.765 --> 00:29:07.750
And we know that this is
going to be non-negative

00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:10.570
in the dual norm, right?

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:13.210
And then it turns out
the gap is the same.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:14.793
This used to be--
if you can think

00:29:14.793 --> 00:29:16.210
about this for the
types of things

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.210
if you had-- if the constraints
do not involve generalized

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:23.100
or vector inequalities,
each of these is 1, right?

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:25.093
And so this term
is just m over t.

00:29:25.093 --> 00:29:26.760
That's the term we
were just looking at.

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.380
It's the m over t
from over there.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:30.090
But now instead
of m, you replace

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:32.910
m, not the number
of constraints,

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:35.220
but actually the
sum of the degree--

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:37.770
the total sum of degrees, right?

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:40.080
Which is actually
not obvious, right?

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.380
Because if I give you a single
semi-definite constraint with

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:46.410
a 10 by 10 matrix, how many--

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:48.810
and I ask you how many
constraints is that?

00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:50.145
It's not clear.

00:29:50.145 --> 00:29:53.010
It's a single vector
constraint, right,

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:55.320
where it's a positive
semidefinite matrix.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:57.343
Or you could say,
well, it's like n, n

00:29:57.343 --> 00:29:59.010
plus 1 over 2 constraints
because that's

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:01.290
the number of scalars
in a symmetric 10 by 10

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:02.670
matrix, right?

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:04.683
But 55 is not right
either because I

00:30:04.683 --> 00:30:05.850
think that's what you'd get.

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:10.400
And what this says is for
this purposes, it's 10.

00:30:10.400 --> 00:30:13.820
It acts as if a
semidefinite inequality

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:18.920
with a 10 by 10 matrix is
roughly equivalent to 10 scalar

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:19.670
inequalities.

00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:21.690
That's what this is saying here.

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:22.190
OK.

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:26.860
So now you get cool stuff.

00:30:26.860 --> 00:30:30.520
You get semidefinite
programming.

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:34.570
You work out what
the log barrier is.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:36.820
You work out what it
is on the central path.

00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:38.470
It's this.

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:39.760
You get dual points.

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:41.710
It's this thing.

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:45.520
And this is the dual of
this semidefinite program.

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:48.530
And the duality gap
is exactly p over t. p

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:51.670
is equal to the size of
these matrices, right?

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:55.105
Because that's the
degree of the logarithm

00:30:55.105 --> 00:30:58.040
on the positive definite cone.

00:30:58.040 --> 00:30:59.867
OK.

00:30:59.867 --> 00:31:01.450
Well, now you have
the barrier method.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:02.410
Here it is.

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:03.920
You look at it for a minute.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:07.150
And here's what you'll find out.

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:09.670
Nothing changed
at least in form.

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:10.690
Nothing changed.

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:12.850
Oh, the only thing was
you had to replace m.

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:14.380
This used to be m.

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:17.110
And now it's some of the
theta i's over t, right?

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:19.540
So in something
like Julia, it would

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:22.120
mean that your code from
before would actually

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:24.055
probably just work.

00:31:24.055 --> 00:31:25.180
Literally just work, right?

00:31:25.180 --> 00:31:27.055
I mean, you go back and
you have to make sure

00:31:27.055 --> 00:31:29.530
that a logarithm is defined
correctly and all that,

00:31:29.530 --> 00:31:31.090
but it would just work.

00:31:31.090 --> 00:31:34.120
And you can see, it's
just exactly the same.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.000
And you get the-- the
complexity analysis just

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:38.860
applies just immediately.

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:39.460
It just works.

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:41.560
Except wherever I
said m over here,

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:44.800
you replace it with sum theta i.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:45.300
OK?

00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:48.990
So this is pretty cool.

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:51.235
And, I mean, maybe
more important

00:31:51.235 --> 00:31:52.860
at least certainly
more important to me

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:55.410
is that it actually--
it actually works

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:57.270
super well in practice.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:01.380
So what's crazy is if
you look at these things,

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:04.500
they look exactly like
the ones we saw before

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:08.160
for linear programs, quadratic
programs, geometric programs.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:10.065
They look the same, right?

00:32:10.065 --> 00:32:12.930
That everything's
over and somewhere

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:16.680
between 20 and 50 steps if
you choose mu reasonably.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:17.980
You see all sorts of stuff.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:20.230
I mean, you couldn't tell
any difference between this.

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:22.230
This plot looks exact--
these look exactly

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:27.780
like they did for
solving an LP, right?

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:30.570
So it's just the same.

00:32:30.570 --> 00:32:34.965
And the fancier methods,
I'll talk about them briefly.

00:32:34.965 --> 00:32:39.210
But they would come in
between 20 and 50 just always.

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:40.470
That's how that works, right?

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:43.350
So by the way, this is pretty
cool because I started--

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:45.270
I started using
semidefinite programming

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.050
for problems in control.

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:50.260
Actually, before the
advent of these methods and

00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:52.540
we used very fancy--

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:55.840
complicated methods that
would take 10 minutes

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:59.380
to solve a semidefinite
program with a handful

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:02.930
of matrix inequalities
that were like 10 by 10.

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:05.770
And we were happy and
we liked the results.

00:33:05.770 --> 00:33:09.550
We had absolutely no idea
that within a couple of years,

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:10.600
there would be methods--

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:15.925
there would be methods that
would be 20x shorter the code

00:33:15.925 --> 00:33:18.750
and be 100x or 1,000x faster.

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.970
Just did not like it was
a-- anyway, people now,

00:33:21.970 --> 00:33:23.390
they just don't--
they don't care.

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:24.760
As a matter of fact,
most people don't even

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:26.135
care how these
things are solved.

00:33:26.135 --> 00:33:30.190
They just call a solver
or, for that matter,

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:33.040
a higher level of ignorance,
which is totally cool and fine

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:35.267
is you just use CVXPY and
call the solve method.

00:33:35.267 --> 00:33:36.850
And you have absolutely
no idea what's

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:39.520
being done on your behalf at
the far end, which is just fine.

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:41.270
That's the way it's
supposed to be, right?

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:43.480
So OK.

00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:48.370
So this shows you this works.

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:50.020
There's like just a same story.

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:53.890
It just takes between
20 and 50 steps.

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.260
OK.

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:57.910
So I do want to
mention something

00:33:57.910 --> 00:34:05.140
which is that the barrier method
is just a simple first cut.

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:07.720
I think yours is probably going
to come in at around 50 lines

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:08.469
order of magnitude.

00:34:08.469 --> 00:34:10.136
If you put a bunch
of comments in there,

00:34:10.136 --> 00:34:11.967
it might be 70 or
something, right?

00:34:11.967 --> 00:34:14.259
If you were to double that
length, you would actually--

00:34:14.259 --> 00:34:17.139
you could actually get
something that would be--

00:34:17.139 --> 00:34:19.780
that would actually
be competitive, right?

00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:23.860
So if you went to 100 lines
or something like that.

00:34:23.860 --> 00:34:25.840
People don't really
use the barrier method

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:27.940
that you've put together,
but they put things--

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:31.659
but what are you-- what is used,
for example, in Ekos or in any

00:34:31.659 --> 00:34:34.330
of these things in all
these commercial tools

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:36.659
as well, what are
used are what are

00:34:36.659 --> 00:34:38.409
called primal dual
interior point methods.

00:34:38.409 --> 00:34:40.480
I'll just say a
little bit about them

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:43.540
just so you know what they are.

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:46.210
So they actually update
the primal dual variables

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:47.360
at each iteration.

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:49.360
And you don't have the
distinction between inner

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:51.280
and-- you don't have
inner-- you're not

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.364
going all the way back to
the central path, right?

00:34:54.364 --> 00:34:55.989
What you'll find out
is if you actually

00:34:55.989 --> 00:34:57.906
look at some of these
codes, you will find out

00:34:57.906 --> 00:35:01.370
that you're still solving
a KKT system at every step.

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:03.535
So that part is
exactly the same.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:06.930
And let's see.

00:35:06.930 --> 00:35:11.820
I should say that they do use
something like what you're

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:16.020
using because your
centering method is actually

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:17.070
an infeasible start.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:19.320
These also use infeasible start.

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:22.410
They use something called
the primal-dual homogeneous

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:22.950
embedding.

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:24.325
We're not going
to talk about it,

00:35:24.325 --> 00:35:25.560
but it's actually very cool.

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:28.260
It's actually it's a
Stanford development.

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:31.755
It's from [? NEA, ?]
maybe 20 years ago.

00:35:31.755 --> 00:35:36.030
It is now used for all solvers.

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:37.470
Commercial, noncommercial.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:38.190
Everywhere.

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:39.550
That is what's used.

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:41.670
And what it does is it
actually solves the primal

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:43.740
and the dual simultaneously.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:46.260
It handles things
like infeasibility

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:49.830
and it handles
unbounded-- unboundedness.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:52.740
All of it just by solving
one-- it solves one problem.

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:54.660
You look at two
scalar variables.

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:58.050
From them, you figure out if
1 is 1 or something like that.

00:35:58.050 --> 00:36:00.840
If 1 is positive, it means you
found a primal-dual solution.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:02.760
If the other is
positive, it means

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:04.860
it's either unbounded or--

00:36:04.860 --> 00:36:06.490
it will actually
get the certificate.

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:09.760
It'll either give you
a ray that establishes

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:14.050
that it is unbounded below or
it'll give you a certificate

00:36:14.050 --> 00:36:16.062
showing that the original
