00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:35.130
Any questions about it?

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:35.790
It's cool.

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:39.600
So actually now, the whole
stack is demystified for you

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:41.010
because here's what it does.

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:46.650
When you type in CVXPY,
CVXPY does forms a sequence

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.390
of equivalent problems.

00:39:48.390 --> 00:39:49.920
It starts with your problem.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:53.610
It does a reduction, makes it
a completely equivalent problem

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:55.845
with a retrieval method, right?

00:39:55.845 --> 00:39:57.780
Then reduces it again and again.

00:39:57.780 --> 00:40:01.650
Maybe 12, 15, 20 reductions
later, it looks up and it says,

00:40:01.650 --> 00:40:06.210
I have a form that one
of my solvers can solve.

00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.280
Everybody following this?

00:40:08.280 --> 00:40:10.920
Then it passes it
off to the solver.

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:14.250
They are not all
interior point methods.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:16.210
SCS, for example, is not.

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:17.985
OSQP is not.

00:40:17.985 --> 00:40:20.340
But many others are.

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:26.040
So Ekos, Clarabel, commercial
ones are most almost entirely--

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:28.050
I guess almost entirely.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:30.637
They are barrier and interior
point methods, right?

00:40:30.637 --> 00:40:32.220
And now you know at
least a little bit

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:33.700
of the details of those.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:35.905
If you go down,
drill down deeper,

00:40:35.905 --> 00:40:39.940
what you know is if you
profile one of these things,

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:44.040
it is only doing the
following, linear algebra.

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:46.230
Absolutely nothing else.

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:49.710
Linear algebra it's doing is
actually mostly just exploiting

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:51.430
sparsity and that's it.

00:40:51.430 --> 00:40:54.630
So now, in a weird way,
you know everything

00:40:54.630 --> 00:40:56.910
at least for the
full stack from what

00:40:56.910 --> 00:41:01.440
happens when you call-- when
you type in myprob.solve.

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:02.730
And now you know.

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:03.840
So yeah?

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:07.170
Is there a difference between
saying interior point methods

00:41:07.170 --> 00:41:09.480
or barrier methods?

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:10.170
Yeah, let's see.

00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:11.760
No, not really.

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:15.670
So barrier method I
think is an older term.

00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:18.160
I like it because it's
a cool retro term.

00:41:18.160 --> 00:41:20.090
But it refers to the same--

00:41:20.090 --> 00:41:22.480
Yeah, it's usually
pretty much the same.

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:23.090
So yeah.

00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:25.940
So to your point I think is
what most people would call it

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:29.520
these days so.

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:32.320
I mean, it's still--
you have to admit,

00:41:32.320 --> 00:41:33.870
it's pretty stunning
that you can

00:41:33.870 --> 00:41:38.475
solve an LP with 10,000
variables and 5,000 constraints

00:41:38.475 --> 00:41:40.620
in 20 iterations.

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:44.040
And that's too extremely
high precision, right?

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:45.730
That's very weird, right?

00:41:45.730 --> 00:41:50.880
Because basically you want
to find a particular vertex

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:53.475
in this polyhedron
of which there's

00:41:53.475 --> 00:41:57.030
a number equal to the number
of subatomic particles

00:41:57.030 --> 00:41:59.130
in the universe, including
dark matter, right?

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:03.480
So that's-- and what it does
is it basically stops and asks

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:06.670
for directions 20 times.

00:42:06.670 --> 00:42:09.760
That's, to me, very weird.

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:11.238
I mean, it's cool.

00:42:11.238 --> 00:42:13.530
We're all very lucky because
we're beneficiaries of it.

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:16.980
But it's very cool, right?

00:42:16.980 --> 00:42:18.160
I still don't get it.

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:21.540
There are people who claim
to understand why this works,

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:24.292
but I don't believe
them so or at least I

00:42:24.292 --> 00:42:25.250
didn't get [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:25.250 --> 00:42:26.040
Yes?

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:29.850
Can you give an example of
how CVXPY reduces the problem

00:42:29.850 --> 00:42:32.020
or comes up with [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:32.020 --> 00:42:32.520
Sure.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:35.100
It's actually--
So we did a lot--

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:36.570
we did those very early on.

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:38.850
I don't know if you
remember we did that.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:41.460
What happens is you actually
don't do a lot of those

00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:44.730
now because it's done for you.

00:42:44.730 --> 00:42:46.590
But the reduction would
be like introduction

00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:49.170
of a slack variable, right?

00:42:49.170 --> 00:42:50.790
It would be--

00:42:50.790 --> 00:42:55.142
I mean, the interesting
ones would be how to handle.

00:42:55.142 --> 00:42:56.600
I mean, an interesting
one would be

00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:59.420
how do we handle the
sum of the 3.5 largest

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:00.860
entries of a vector?

00:43:00.860 --> 00:43:02.300
That's a convex function.

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:06.260
Since CVXPY sum
largest x comma 3.5.

00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:08.600
Just you don't have to
think about anything.

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:10.880
That's a much more
interesting reduction.

00:43:10.880 --> 00:43:14.720
Those things you-- all of those
you would know about, right?

00:43:14.720 --> 00:43:17.060
So there's no-- most
of the reductions

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:19.490
are actually really dumb.

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:20.660
They're really boring.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:21.470
Here's one.

00:43:21.470 --> 00:43:24.920
You want to maximize
an objective, right?

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:26.120
Well, none of our--

00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:28.320
no solver solves that problem.

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:30.290
So here's an equivalent problem.

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:31.040
Ready?

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:33.380
Minimize minus the objective.

00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:34.580
There you go.

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:36.380
I mean, that's a reduction.

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:38.163
It's dumb, right?

00:43:38.163 --> 00:43:39.830
And then the retrieval
method is simple.

00:43:39.830 --> 00:43:44.060
The retrieval method is that
you just replicate x, right?

00:43:44.060 --> 00:43:44.990
The solution.

00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:48.740
But you change the optimal value
by flipping the sign, right?

00:43:48.740 --> 00:43:51.800
So they range between-- these
reductions range between--

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:55.040
most of them are deeply boring
like the one I just said.

00:43:55.040 --> 00:43:58.690
And a handful of them
are actually interesting.

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:00.780
So that was a non-answer.

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:04.520
But you have seen these in
maybe week four of the course.

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:05.190
These--

00:44:05.190 --> 00:44:06.566
I guess what I was
asking, like, how do you

00:44:06.566 --> 00:44:08.565
tell which one
[? is useful ?] [INAUDIBLE]??

00:44:08.565 --> 00:44:09.360
Ah, OK.

00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:11.280
Well, now that's
another story, right?

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:15.060
So it actually finds a shortest
path in the graph, right?

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:17.730
So it takes a problem.

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:20.220
It estimates what is
the most specific.

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:22.080
I mean, this just
because you asked, right?

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:24.190
It looks at your
problem and says,

00:44:24.190 --> 00:44:28.830
I could compile that to a QP
and a second-order cone program

00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:29.850
or something like that.

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:33.600
Then it looks among your solvers
unless you specified one.

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:36.280
It looks for the one
that's most specific.

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:40.110
In that case, it might pick
the QP or something like that.

00:44:40.110 --> 00:44:41.910
So then it actually
has a shortest path.

00:44:41.910 --> 00:44:44.610
It tries to figure out,
is there a sequence

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:48.015
of transformations that will
take this problem to a QP?

00:44:48.015 --> 00:44:50.870
So that's roughly what it does.

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:53.990
So cool.

00:44:53.990 --> 00:44:55.175
OK.

00:44:55.175 --> 00:44:58.790
So weirdly, I think now
