00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:26.475
OK.

00:46:26.475 --> 00:46:28.510
So here's the idea.

00:46:28.510 --> 00:46:31.230
So a cardinality, by the
way, is simply the number

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:33.330
of non-zeros of a vector.

00:46:33.330 --> 00:46:36.570
And, in fact, it's a
separable function, right?

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:40.560
So it actually is based on this.

00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:45.390
It's based on-- so it's
the cardinality-- this

00:46:45.390 --> 00:46:50.770
is for a scalar of let's
say a is equal to either 1

00:46:50.770 --> 00:46:52.470
if a is not 0.

00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:54.670
And 0, if a equals 0.

00:46:54.670 --> 00:46:55.170
OK?

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:56.100
So that's--

00:46:56.100 --> 00:47:01.590
And if you sum this function
over all the coefficients

00:47:01.590 --> 00:47:03.390
of a vector, you
get the cardinality,

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:04.980
which is the number, right?

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:07.710
And this function,
obviously, is not convex.

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:08.970
I guess that's obvious.

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:10.920
It looks like this.

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:11.970
That's empty.

00:47:11.970 --> 00:47:13.260
And that, oop.

00:47:13.260 --> 00:47:13.770
Yeah.

00:47:13.770 --> 00:47:14.270
Whoops.

00:47:14.270 --> 00:47:14.790
OK yeah.

00:47:14.790 --> 00:47:15.940
It looks like this.

00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:19.320
And then this is looks
like it's this point.

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:19.922
And wait.

00:47:19.922 --> 00:47:20.880
No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:47:20.880 --> 00:47:23.670
Here, it's this and then that.

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:26.280
So this is-- or if you
like, I'll make it look

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:28.170
like that in a very
weird way, right?

00:47:28.170 --> 00:47:30.420
So that's the
cardinality function.

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:32.631
OK.

00:47:32.631 --> 00:47:34.980
Obviously not convex, right?

00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:37.430
OK.

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:40.610
So the history of this
goes back in way pre-date--

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:43.250
it goes back into
the '50s at the least

00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:45.090
and maybe even earlier.

00:47:45.090 --> 00:47:48.830
But it's used in all cool things
like in sparse design is one.

00:47:48.830 --> 00:47:54.110
So a very interesting
example of that is it

00:47:54.110 --> 00:47:57.590
was actually used in aerospace
in design of structures

00:47:57.590 --> 00:47:59.900
in the 1950s and '60s.

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:02.150
So let me explain
how that works.

00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:03.560
You're going to
build a structure

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:07.550
and you put in all these bars
that connect two nodes, right?

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:10.227
So it's a space frame
or something like that.

00:48:10.227 --> 00:48:12.560
By the way, how many people
know what I'm talking about?

00:48:12.560 --> 00:48:14.418
Because if-- a couple, OK.

00:48:14.418 --> 00:48:15.210
That's good enough.

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:18.155
And the rest should just pick
up the flavor of it, right?

00:48:18.155 --> 00:48:19.530
So I have a whole
bunch of nodes.

00:48:19.530 --> 00:48:21.750
And I'm going to
put in bars, right?

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:24.390
And my variables actually
are the cross sectional areas

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:26.190
of the bars, OK?

00:48:26.190 --> 00:48:27.100
That's the variables.

00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:31.890
But what you do is the bars--
you put in a gazillion bars,

00:48:31.890 --> 00:48:35.070
you have absolutely no
intention to use a million--

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:37.740
a million bars in your
space frame, right?

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:40.290
This could be, for example,
the wing of an airplane, right?

00:48:40.290 --> 00:48:45.510
Or some structure like that, or
some structure in a spacecraft,

00:48:45.510 --> 00:48:46.740
right?

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:47.310
OK.

00:48:47.310 --> 00:48:52.800
So what you do then is you
actually put an L1 norm

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:57.040
on the cross sectional areas.

00:48:57.040 --> 00:48:59.040
What's the L1 norm of
a non-negative vector?

00:49:04.856 --> 00:49:06.170
[INAUDIBLE]

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:06.770
Thank you.

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:07.932
Yeah, OK, fine.

00:49:07.932 --> 00:49:09.890
So you could actually--
what's weird about that

00:49:09.890 --> 00:49:12.290
is you could look in some
of those papers for L1 norm.

00:49:12.290 --> 00:49:14.850
And you're like I don't
see any L1 norm here.

00:49:14.850 --> 00:49:18.260
And the reason is the
things that you're computing

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:20.618
in L1 norm of are non-negative.

00:49:20.618 --> 00:49:21.410
So it's just a sum.

00:49:21.410 --> 00:49:22.220
OK, fine.

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:23.240
All right.

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:26.150
So what happens
is you solve this

00:49:26.150 --> 00:49:30.180
and the constraints are
you'd have a limit on weight.

00:49:30.180 --> 00:49:30.950
You have a limit.

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:32.780
What you also have is a
whole bunch of constraints

00:49:32.780 --> 00:49:34.040
which are performance related.

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:36.170
And it basically says
if I take this structure

00:49:36.170 --> 00:49:38.420
and I put various forces
on various places,

00:49:38.420 --> 00:49:39.950
can it withstand it, right?

00:49:39.950 --> 00:49:42.793
I might have a maximum stress
limit in each bar or something.

00:49:42.793 --> 00:49:44.960
Everybody-- so there's a
whole bunch of constraints.

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:49.820
The comment is strong enough
to handle boost, takeoff,

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:50.900
whatever reentry.

00:49:50.900 --> 00:49:52.520
I don't care whatever
this is, right?

00:49:52.520 --> 00:49:54.002
Or rough turbulence.

00:49:54.002 --> 00:49:54.960
All that kind of stuff.

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:56.002
Everybody following this?

00:49:56.002 --> 00:49:57.940
So that's a whole
bunch of constraints.

00:49:57.940 --> 00:50:01.370
Anyway, when you
solve this problem,

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:03.370
you offered it the option
of using-- they didn't

00:50:03.370 --> 00:50:05.140
do a million in the '60s, OK?

00:50:05.140 --> 00:50:06.580
But now they do
a million, right?

00:50:06.580 --> 00:50:10.330
So you offered it the
choice of a million bars.

00:50:10.330 --> 00:50:15.040
And what happens is 322
turn out to be positive

00:50:15.040 --> 00:50:16.180
and the rest are zero.

00:50:16.180 --> 00:50:17.320
Everybody got this?

00:50:17.320 --> 00:50:19.490
And then it's cool because
someone said, well,

00:50:19.490 --> 00:50:20.365
what did you just do?

00:50:20.365 --> 00:50:23.500
And you say I just designed--

00:50:23.500 --> 00:50:24.940
I just designed the structure.

00:50:24.940 --> 00:50:25.930
It's a cool structure.

00:50:25.930 --> 00:50:27.940
And you see all these bars
connecting various points

00:50:27.940 --> 00:50:28.820
and stuff like that.

00:50:28.820 --> 00:50:29.980
And if your specs
were right it would

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:31.750
be a completely
reasonable structure.

00:50:31.750 --> 00:50:35.220
And someone said that sounds
like a combinatorial problem,

00:50:35.220 --> 00:50:35.790
right?

00:50:35.790 --> 00:50:36.563
Because you did--

00:50:36.563 --> 00:50:37.980
Here's what you
did not solve, you

00:50:37.980 --> 00:50:44.610
didn't say I want
322 bars from my--

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:47.460
what I'm offering you which
is 1.1 million, right?

00:50:47.460 --> 00:50:52.320
Because 1.1 million choose 322
is a super big number, right?

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:53.770
So you did not
solve that problem.

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:57.030
So this is a heuristic,
but that's what happens.

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:58.600
Everybody got that?

00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:00.180
So that's already in the '60s.

00:51:00.180 --> 00:51:02.710
People were doing
this, which is cool.

00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:05.085
I know that there was
a great example in--

00:51:05.085 --> 00:51:06.540
Can they do it in circuits?

00:51:06.540 --> 00:51:07.290
That's a good one.

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:08.910
So, in circuit
design, you're going

00:51:08.910 --> 00:51:11.800
to build yourself a filter.

00:51:11.800 --> 00:51:13.800
You're going to build,
let's say, an FIR filter.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:16.355
So I'm speaking
dialect, but it's OK.

00:51:16.355 --> 00:51:16.980
If people get--

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:19.410
So you're going to build
yourself a finite impulse

00:51:19.410 --> 00:51:22.830
response filter, which is a set
of a bunch of sums and stuff

00:51:22.830 --> 00:51:23.910
like that.

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:25.920
And you design the coefficients.

00:51:25.920 --> 00:51:28.270
Here are the coefficients--
can be negative.

00:51:28.270 --> 00:51:30.600
So there's an L1 norm put in.

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:34.050
And you say I want a filter
that has the following specs,

00:51:34.050 --> 00:51:36.280
but I also want the
coefficients to be sparse.

00:51:36.280 --> 00:51:38.140
So you add an L1 term.

00:51:38.140 --> 00:51:39.580
It's sparse.

00:51:39.580 --> 00:51:41.380
And it's very cool
because then when

00:51:41.380 --> 00:51:44.230
you're going to emit
the hardware for it.

00:51:44.230 --> 00:51:46.592
When a coefficient is zero,
it means that little block

00:51:46.592 --> 00:51:48.550
in your actual hardware
circuit, you don't even

00:51:48.550 --> 00:51:50.980
need it because I know how to--

00:51:50.980 --> 00:51:53.410
I'm not a hardware designer,
but I do know how to--

00:51:53.410 --> 00:51:56.740
I do know how to synthesize
hardware that multiplies by 0

00:51:56.740 --> 00:51:59.830
and adds it to the result.
That I can do, right?

00:51:59.830 --> 00:52:01.750
And the answer is just
no hardware at all.

00:52:01.750 --> 00:52:03.422
Everybody following these?

00:52:03.422 --> 00:52:05.380
And then, of course, I
think most-- many of you

00:52:05.380 --> 00:52:08.980
have seen this in statistics.

00:52:08.980 --> 00:52:12.610
Actually for two different
purposes at least two.

00:52:12.610 --> 00:52:17.650
The first is for sparse features
is feature selection, right?

00:52:17.650 --> 00:52:22.690
So you'd say here's
a bunch of data.

00:52:22.690 --> 00:52:25.180
I have 500 patients.

00:52:25.180 --> 00:52:29.530
250 have one type of cancer, 250
don't or something like that.

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:31.300
Here's gene activation data.

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:36.610
So I give you, I don't know,
80,000 gene expression levels.

00:52:36.610 --> 00:52:38.800
And then if you put L1--

00:52:38.800 --> 00:52:42.310
if you put an L1
regularizer on, let's say

00:52:42.310 --> 00:52:45.340
just logistic regression
to classify the two,

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:49.810
then it's going to go in
and choose 22 genes, right?

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:50.570
Everybody?

00:52:50.570 --> 00:52:53.650
So, by the way, how many people
have seen that or something

00:52:53.650 --> 00:52:56.725
like that in some class?

00:52:56.725 --> 00:52:58.480
Where was it?

00:52:58.480 --> 00:53:00.280
Like Stat 315 or something?

00:53:00.280 --> 00:53:01.030
206.

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:02.680
206?

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:04.060
Good.

00:53:04.060 --> 00:53:07.250
I was hoping for more people to
raise their hands on that one.

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:09.130
These are super
cool things, right?

00:53:09.130 --> 00:53:11.210
So everybody should
have seen these things.

00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:13.588
But anyway, do you
know what they do?

00:53:13.588 --> 00:53:14.630
I have biologist friends.

00:53:14.630 --> 00:53:15.963
You know what they do with that?

00:53:15.963 --> 00:53:18.340
So I have a biologist
friend who hates math.

00:53:18.340 --> 00:53:19.000
Doesn't trust.

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:19.833
He says it's stupid.

00:53:19.833 --> 00:53:20.920
It doesn't work.

00:53:20.920 --> 00:53:23.470
I caught him actually
running one of those things.

00:53:23.470 --> 00:53:25.870
I was like you're so busted.

00:53:25.870 --> 00:53:26.392
He said no.

00:53:26.392 --> 00:53:27.100
I don't trust it.

00:53:27.100 --> 00:53:28.517
He said what I do
is I take the 22

00:53:28.517 --> 00:53:32.535
genes, I look at the first 10
and go like duh, of course,

00:53:32.535 --> 00:53:34.660
they're related to this
pathway because he actually

00:53:34.660 --> 00:53:35.243
knows biology.

00:53:35.243 --> 00:53:37.150
He said, of course,
that's obviously related

00:53:37.150 --> 00:53:38.493
to the pathway for x, y, and z.

00:53:38.493 --> 00:53:39.910
And he looked at
two others and he

00:53:39.910 --> 00:53:42.310
says that has nothing to do
with-- that's ridiculous.

00:53:42.310 --> 00:53:44.830
And he looks at three others
and he goes like, hmm.

00:53:44.830 --> 00:53:48.340
These are people who know
genes by name, right?

00:53:48.340 --> 00:53:52.380
He looks at them and he goes
like, hmm, that's interesting.

00:53:52.380 --> 00:53:54.220
That actually could be related.

00:53:54.220 --> 00:53:54.803
That could be.

00:53:54.803 --> 00:53:56.595
And then I said, well,
what do you do then?

00:53:56.595 --> 00:53:58.610
He said, well, then
I just call my lab.

00:53:58.610 --> 00:54:00.980
He said and he makes his
postdocs go in and knock out

00:54:00.980 --> 00:54:03.525
those genes and see if
something happens, right?

00:54:03.525 --> 00:54:05.150
Then the next morning
maybe it happens,

00:54:05.150 --> 00:54:06.470
maybe it doesn't, right?

00:54:06.470 --> 00:54:09.365
Or whenever the
experiment's over so.

00:54:09.365 --> 00:54:10.490
That's fine, right?

00:54:10.490 --> 00:54:12.260
But OK.

00:54:12.260 --> 00:54:13.130
OK.

00:54:13.130 --> 00:54:15.620
Let's see some other
examples of it.

00:54:15.620 --> 00:54:16.970
Oh, it comes up in--

00:54:16.970 --> 00:54:19.580
Oh, in a lot of image
processing-- signal processing,

00:54:19.580 --> 00:54:22.220
geophysics from the '70s.

00:54:22.220 --> 00:54:24.910
Oh, there's on total
variation reconstruction.

00:54:24.910 --> 00:54:25.910
We've already seen that.

00:54:25.910 --> 00:54:28.340
That's actually
basically an L1 norm

00:54:28.340 --> 00:54:31.940
applied to a derivative
either a first difference

00:54:31.940 --> 00:54:35.990
or, for example, in an image,
it might be to a gradient,

00:54:35.990 --> 00:54:38.550
in a space gradient in an image.

00:54:38.550 --> 00:54:39.050
OK.

00:54:39.050 --> 00:54:40.280
So these are--

00:54:40.280 --> 00:54:41.972
And yeah, that's a bit old.

00:54:41.972 --> 00:54:44.180
I mean, yeah, there are some
theoretical results that

00:54:44.180 --> 00:54:45.620
guarantee the methods works.

00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:48.890
I mean, that's I think
it's cool, but whatever.

00:54:48.890 --> 00:54:49.820
It's fine.

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:52.760
They work without these
methods anyway so.

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:54.050
OK.

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:57.680
So what we're going to
do now is look at this

00:54:57.680 --> 00:55:02.000
and go through this stuff
a bit more carefully.

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:04.430
So the cardinality
is the function

00:55:04.430 --> 00:55:05.510
I just plotted over here.

00:55:05.510 --> 00:55:07.580
It looks like that.

00:55:07.580 --> 00:55:08.930
It's quasi-concave.

00:55:08.930 --> 00:55:10.190
But, I mean, who cares?

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:13.190
Because that's not something
that tells you congratulations.

00:55:13.190 --> 00:55:18.320
You can maximize the cardinality
of a vector, which is silly.

00:55:18.320 --> 00:55:23.190
Otherwise, it has no
convexity properties, right?

00:55:23.190 --> 00:55:25.980
You have lots of interesting
problems like a convex

00:55:25.980 --> 00:55:26.850
cardinality problem.

00:55:26.850 --> 00:55:29.410
Here's one that looks like this.

00:55:29.410 --> 00:55:31.860
So this says here's
a set of convex.

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:34.170
Here's a convex set, say.

00:55:34.170 --> 00:55:39.210
And what you're going to do
is you say please find in this

00:55:39.210 --> 00:55:43.410
set the point with the
smallest number of nonzeros.

00:55:43.410 --> 00:55:46.380
This is general, but we're going
to look at specific examples.

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:48.990
And you'll see this is a
super interesting problem.

00:55:48.990 --> 00:55:50.820
Or you could do
things like this.

00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:53.625
I could have a standard
convex problem here.

00:55:53.625 --> 00:55:54.135
Here.

00:55:54.135 --> 00:55:55.650
But I could add
a constraint that

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:59.580
says you can only use
k nonzeros, right?

00:55:59.580 --> 00:56:04.440
So I mean in statistics
or machine learning,

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:08.880
this would be maximize
my log likelihood

00:56:08.880 --> 00:56:11.610
subject to some constraints
that are required.

00:56:11.610 --> 00:56:14.730
Then also-- P.S.,
you're only allowed

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:17.940
to use-- you can't use
more than 22 features

00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:20.040
and I'm giving you
a million, right?

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:21.690
So that would be that, right.

00:56:21.690 --> 00:56:23.680
And there it's called
like feature selection.

00:56:23.680 --> 00:56:25.650
We'll get to that.

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:26.475
OK.

00:56:26.475 --> 00:56:30.490
So just a couple of
observations about this.

00:56:30.490 --> 00:56:34.380
So the first one is if you
fix the sparsity pattern,

00:56:34.380 --> 00:56:36.540
then you just get a
convex problem, right?

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:41.513
So if I tell you fit my
model and you go, OK.

00:56:41.513 --> 00:56:42.930
You say I have a
million features.

00:56:42.930 --> 00:56:46.020
You say, oh, but only these
22 are allowed to be nonzero

00:56:46.020 --> 00:56:47.130
and all the rest are zero.

00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:49.080
Then you're like, OK,
because then it's just

00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:50.580
a problem with 22 variables.

00:56:50.580 --> 00:56:51.630
Everybody got this?

00:56:51.630 --> 00:56:54.060
So this is obvious.

00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:59.270
That means that you could solve
2 to the n convex problems

00:56:59.270 --> 00:57:00.420
to solve these, right?

00:57:00.420 --> 00:57:02.070
If n is 1,000, it's not.

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:03.180
I mean, if n-- sorry.

00:57:03.180 --> 00:57:04.950
n is 100, it's not--

00:57:04.950 --> 00:57:08.070
n is less than 10,
this is not-- this

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:10.680
might not be insane, right?

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:12.180
So yeah.

00:57:12.180 --> 00:57:15.870
So that's one.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:19.380
But this brute force
search doesn't work for--

00:57:19.380 --> 00:57:22.997
obviously for bigger problems.

00:57:22.997 --> 00:57:25.080
It's extremely easy to
show that the whole thing--

00:57:25.080 --> 00:57:27.153
that this general
problem is (NP-) hard.

00:57:27.153 --> 00:57:28.570
I think we'll do
that in a minute.

00:57:28.570 --> 00:57:30.240
And you could solve
it globally by what

00:57:30.240 --> 00:57:32.310
are called branch
and bound methods.

00:57:32.310 --> 00:57:34.170
And there's also
branch and cut methods.

00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:35.670
There's all sorts
of names for this.

00:57:35.670 --> 00:57:38.070
It's a whole area I guess
people would call it.

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:41.394
Like mixed integer
convex programming.

00:57:41.394 --> 00:57:43.170
They might.

00:57:43.170 --> 00:57:44.310
These things can work.

00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:46.260
Some of them can
work spectacularly.

00:57:46.260 --> 00:57:48.320
I'm not going to
talk about those.

00:57:48.320 --> 00:57:52.980
But anyway, just general
ideas about how you would

00:57:52.980 --> 00:57:54.270
solve these problems, right?

00:57:57.630 --> 00:58:00.990
So you can actually
express Boolean LP

00:58:00.990 --> 00:58:03.972
as a cardinality problem
like that, right?

00:58:03.972 --> 00:58:06.180
And I think you've actually
done a couple of problems

00:58:06.180 --> 00:58:08.250
on heuristic-- first
of all, getting

00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:12.090
computing bounds for
Boolean LPs and then also

00:58:12.090 --> 00:58:17.640
for heuristics for actually
approximately solving them.

00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:21.150
I believe you had that
in some homework, right?

00:58:21.150 --> 00:58:24.100
So the way that works is you
want to solve this problem.

00:58:24.100 --> 00:58:27.150
Now, by the way, solving a
set of linear inequalities

00:58:27.150 --> 00:58:31.146
with Boolean variables
is basically like all--

00:58:31.146 --> 00:58:33.600
all of these problems
in computer science.

00:58:33.600 --> 00:58:35.940
Like everything is that, right?

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:37.440
It's things like 3-SAT.

00:58:37.440 --> 00:58:38.940
It's the Traveling
Salesman Problem.

00:58:38.940 --> 00:58:41.085
I can write down very
short descriptions

00:58:41.085 --> 00:58:44.217
with 0, 1 variables.

00:58:44.217 --> 00:58:46.800
And you just write it this way
and it's a cardinality problem.

00:58:46.800 --> 00:58:47.730
And it just looks like this.

00:58:47.730 --> 00:58:49.320
It's actually the
cardinality of x

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:52.820
stacked on 1 minus x is less
than or equal to n, right?

00:58:52.820 --> 00:58:56.610
Because this thing
tells you the number

00:58:56.610 --> 00:58:58.260
of entries of x and the nonzero.

00:58:58.260 --> 00:59:00.210
This tells you the
number of entries

00:59:00.210 --> 00:59:02.610
of x that are not one, right?

00:59:02.610 --> 00:59:04.470
And so if you add
those two together,

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:06.210
the smallest that could be is n.

00:59:06.210 --> 00:59:11.340
And that would be if
every x is either 0 or 1.

00:59:11.340 --> 00:59:14.520
If you throw in an x
that's 3.6, then that's

00:59:14.520 --> 00:59:16.410
going to come out to be
n plus 1 or something

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:18.430
like that, that sum, right?

00:59:18.430 --> 00:59:18.930
OK.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:23.040
So that immediately establishes
that solving convex cardinality

00:59:23.040 --> 00:59:24.960
problems is like
(NP-) hard, right?

00:59:24.960 --> 00:59:27.780
So OK.

00:59:27.780 --> 00:59:29.720
OK.

00:59:29.720 --> 00:59:31.520
So now we'll look
at some examples

00:59:31.520 --> 00:59:34.520
before we get into the methods
because they're very, very

00:59:34.520 --> 00:59:36.350
cool.

00:59:36.350 --> 00:59:36.850
Yeah.

00:59:36.850 --> 00:59:40.480
So sparse design is C
is a set of things that

00:59:40.480 --> 00:59:42.100
give you your specifications.

00:59:42.100 --> 00:59:45.040
It says, here's what
x has to satisfy.

00:59:45.040 --> 00:59:47.440
And then you simply
want the sparsest

00:59:47.440 --> 00:59:52.120
x that satisfies your
conditions, right?

00:59:52.120 --> 00:59:55.330
And the idea there is
presumably a zero value

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:57.640
of x simplifies
the design, right?

00:59:57.640 --> 01:00:01.300
Or generally, it can actually
be components that don't even--

01:00:01.300 --> 01:00:04.600
for example, I think we already
talked about a Truss design.

01:00:04.600 --> 01:00:07.007
And a zero
coefficient is it says

01:00:07.007 --> 01:00:09.340
you should attach a bar between
this node and that wire.

01:00:09.340 --> 01:00:10.810
And what's the
cross sectional area

01:00:10.810 --> 01:00:14.020
zero and you're like,
OK, as in no bar, right?

01:00:14.020 --> 01:00:17.080
For an FIR filter, if
you're designing a filter,

01:00:17.080 --> 01:00:19.300
it says there's whole
blocks that you just don't

01:00:19.300 --> 01:00:20.800
need in the hardware, right?

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:23.290
So that's what that is.

01:00:23.290 --> 01:00:26.450
Oh, it's used in wire sizing,
which is actually really cool.

01:00:26.450 --> 01:00:29.725
So I've seen people use this for
circuit design and wire sizing,

01:00:29.725 --> 01:00:33.370
where they-- they're,
let's say, generating--

01:00:33.370 --> 01:00:36.890
let's say you're synthesizing--
which is their word

01:00:36.890 --> 01:00:39.980
for optimizing--

01:00:39.980 --> 01:00:44.480
a power-- a power distribution
network on a chip, right?

01:00:44.480 --> 01:00:46.880
So what you do is you put
in a whole bunch of wires

01:00:46.880 --> 01:00:47.780
you have no--

01:00:47.780 --> 01:00:50.570
it's like you put in fact
an entire mesh in, right?

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:53.270
You have no intention of
actually implementing a mesh

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:54.930
because it's going
to be too expensive.

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:55.970
So you do that.

01:00:55.970 --> 01:00:57.290
You write down the conditions.

01:00:57.290 --> 01:00:58.915
It's all pretty
straightforward, right?

01:00:58.915 --> 01:01:00.680
There's total amounts
of current that

01:01:00.680 --> 01:01:02.360
have to be go various places.

01:01:02.360 --> 01:01:02.900
And anyway.

01:01:02.900 --> 01:01:05.240
So you do that,
that's going to be C.

01:01:05.240 --> 01:01:07.580
And then your
variables are the--

01:01:07.580 --> 01:01:10.070
actually weirdly that's--
it's the width of the wires.

01:01:10.070 --> 01:01:13.040
They have a constant height so
it's also the cross-- basically

01:01:13.040 --> 01:01:14.420
the cross sectional area.

01:01:14.420 --> 01:01:16.760
And then, when you do
that, what you'll find?

01:01:16.760 --> 01:01:19.250
Is it'll synthesize--
it'll actually

01:01:19.250 --> 01:01:21.240
choose which wires to use.

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:22.970
So you started with
a big mesh and then

01:01:22.970 --> 01:01:23.870
you'll end up with something.

01:01:23.870 --> 01:01:24.830
It has to be connected.

01:01:24.830 --> 01:01:26.960
Because otherwise, you
probably wouldn't end up--

01:01:26.960 --> 01:01:28.460
you wouldn't meet
your specs, right?

01:01:28.460 --> 01:01:30.320
Because one of your specs
is that you power up every

01:01:30.320 --> 01:01:31.530
module on your circuit.

01:01:31.530 --> 01:01:38.508
So how many people know
about that kind of stuff?

01:01:38.508 --> 01:01:40.600
I ain't getting you.

01:01:40.600 --> 01:01:43.270
Giving examples that no
one gets, but that's OK.

01:01:43.270 --> 01:01:48.220
I mean, the basic idea though is
pretty straightforward, right?

01:01:48.220 --> 01:01:50.530
So OK.

01:01:50.530 --> 01:01:53.920
So these are examples
of these things.

01:01:53.920 --> 01:01:54.430
OK.

01:01:54.430 --> 01:01:57.520
Now we get to machine
learning and statistics.

01:01:57.520 --> 01:02:00.100
You have sparse modeling and
regressor selection, right?

01:02:00.100 --> 01:02:03.360
So here, this is not the--

01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:05.287
this is not the
notation that someone

01:02:05.287 --> 01:02:07.120
in statistics or machine
learning would use,

01:02:07.120 --> 01:02:08.500
but too bad.

01:02:08.500 --> 01:02:12.860
So here x, which someone else
might call theta or beta,

01:02:12.860 --> 01:02:17.650
depends on which what your
statistics identity is

01:02:17.650 --> 01:02:20.110
or machine learning identity is.

01:02:20.110 --> 01:02:23.800
But here, you're
simply saying please--

01:02:23.800 --> 01:02:27.250
it says please select k out
of the number of features.

01:02:27.250 --> 01:02:29.230
I guess m, is that right?

01:02:29.230 --> 01:02:29.860
Nope.

01:02:29.860 --> 01:02:31.030
n.

01:02:31.030 --> 01:02:35.980
So please choose k features to
use to fit the model, right?

01:02:35.980 --> 01:02:38.320
So by the way, that's a
very strong and pretty good

01:02:38.320 --> 01:02:41.350
regularizer or something
like that, right?

01:02:41.350 --> 01:02:42.355
OK.

01:02:42.355 --> 01:02:47.230
And I mean, you could do this
by trying all n choose k choices

01:02:47.230 --> 01:02:52.030
and lots of examples people have
things like you could write it

01:02:52.030 --> 01:02:54.310
as a penalized form.

01:02:54.310 --> 01:02:57.340
You could find
the sparsest model

01:02:57.340 --> 01:02:59.110
that gives you a certain--

01:02:59.110 --> 01:03:02.410
that gives you a
certain performance.

01:03:02.410 --> 01:03:04.390
I guess this would be
on your training data.

01:03:04.390 --> 01:03:05.320
Everybody got that?

01:03:05.320 --> 01:03:08.320
So that'd be find me the
sparsest thing that actually

01:03:08.320 --> 01:03:10.900
gives me the following
pre-specified performance

01:03:10.900 --> 01:03:12.640
on the training data.

01:03:12.640 --> 01:03:14.590
So OK.

01:03:14.590 --> 01:03:18.460
So this is the sparse modeling
and regressor selection.

01:03:18.460 --> 01:03:20.110
Sparse signal reconstruction.

01:03:20.110 --> 01:03:22.700
This is a big deal.

01:03:22.700 --> 01:03:25.840
Maybe fading a
little bit, but maybe

01:03:25.840 --> 01:03:28.270
'90s through the
maybe five years ago

01:03:28.270 --> 01:03:31.510
was kind of a big deal.

01:03:31.510 --> 01:03:33.610
And the typical
thing there was this.

01:03:33.610 --> 01:03:35.500
As you'd say a measure--

01:03:35.500 --> 01:03:37.360
what you want to do
is estimate something

01:03:37.360 --> 01:03:42.340
like x, I'll switch to
their dialect, right?

01:03:42.340 --> 01:03:47.440
So a would be a basis, which
drives the columns of a

01:03:47.440 --> 01:03:52.450
would be a basis for the signals
except that, I don't know,

01:03:52.450 --> 01:03:56.020
since 1820 everyone
agreed what a basis was.

01:03:56.020 --> 01:03:57.685
And people in signal
processing decided

01:03:57.685 --> 01:03:59.560
that they would use
their own meaning for it.

01:03:59.560 --> 01:04:03.880
And they just dropped the fact
that they were independent.

01:04:03.880 --> 01:04:05.390
I couldn't make this stuff up.

01:04:05.390 --> 01:04:08.020
And then people challenged
them and they'd say, oh no.

01:04:08.020 --> 01:04:09.280
You're misunderstanding.

01:04:09.280 --> 01:04:12.430
This is an overcomplete
basis, right?

01:04:12.430 --> 01:04:14.980
Which, by the way,
here's another--

01:04:14.980 --> 01:04:17.350
you want to know how you
describe an overcomplete basis?

01:04:17.350 --> 01:04:18.610
It's a set of vectors.

01:04:18.610 --> 01:04:19.360
That's what it is.

01:04:19.360 --> 01:04:20.800
It has no other properties.

01:04:20.800 --> 01:04:23.380
Anyway, don't get
me started on that.

01:04:23.380 --> 01:04:25.930
But they would say so the
columns of A would be-- they

01:04:25.930 --> 01:04:28.540
would call that
their basis, right?

01:04:28.540 --> 01:04:31.900
Even though if they said
that to any normal person

01:04:31.900 --> 01:04:34.432
not in that particular
sub, sub, subfield,

01:04:34.432 --> 01:04:36.640
they would interpret that
they'd say oh, that's cool.

01:04:36.640 --> 01:04:37.700
It's a basis.

01:04:37.700 --> 01:04:38.200
Great.

01:04:38.200 --> 01:04:41.680
Meaning, they're linearly
independent and span.

01:04:41.680 --> 01:04:42.850
It doesn't matter.

01:04:42.850 --> 01:04:43.600
[? You ?] do that.

01:04:43.600 --> 01:04:45.190
And then they
would say the thing

01:04:45.190 --> 01:04:48.050
I'm looking for is
sparse in that basis.

01:04:48.050 --> 01:04:49.700
So you'd hear things like this.

01:04:49.700 --> 01:04:56.150
You'd say, well, I think that
the MRI image I want to recover

01:04:56.150 --> 01:04:59.660
is sparse in this wavelet basis.

01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:02.780
That would be the typical kind
of thing they would say, right?

01:05:02.780 --> 01:05:04.050
And that's fine.

01:05:04.050 --> 01:05:04.550
Anyway.

01:05:04.550 --> 01:05:07.080
So that you'd end up with things
that would look like this.

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:09.020
And that would be
these are generally

01:05:09.020 --> 01:05:11.660
things like sparse
signal reconstruction.

01:05:11.660 --> 01:05:12.515
So OK.

01:05:15.570 --> 01:05:18.510
Another one, which is
actually very interesting,

01:05:18.510 --> 01:05:25.410
this is less often spoken of
I think is handling outliers,

01:05:25.410 --> 01:05:25.950
right?

01:05:25.950 --> 01:05:29.880
This actually
makes sense, right?

01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:32.670
What you should have
in your mind by now--

01:05:32.670 --> 01:05:34.530
and I imagine already do--

01:05:34.530 --> 01:05:38.550
is that when I say
something like L1,

01:05:38.550 --> 01:05:40.327
different things
should come up, right?

01:05:40.327 --> 01:05:41.910
A picture in your
mind should come up.

01:05:41.910 --> 01:05:45.390
As you come up as,
let's say, a sparsifier.

01:05:45.390 --> 01:05:47.160
And what sparsifying about it?

01:05:47.160 --> 01:05:48.840
That point at the bottom.

01:05:48.840 --> 01:05:50.490
Because roughly
speaking, what happens

01:05:50.490 --> 01:05:53.820
is the pressure to
move something to zero

01:05:53.820 --> 01:05:57.050
keeps up until
you're zero, right?

01:05:57.050 --> 01:05:59.240
There's another property of L1.

01:05:59.240 --> 01:06:01.820
You also know that it's a--

01:06:01.820 --> 01:06:04.610
when it's a penalty, it's
something that's robust, right?

01:06:04.610 --> 01:06:06.110
And you might--
because what happens

01:06:06.110 --> 01:06:10.400
is that means that for large
values, it grows slowly.

01:06:10.400 --> 01:06:15.260
It grows as slowly as a
convex penalty can, right?

01:06:15.260 --> 01:06:18.920
And that you would use to do
things like to do sparse stuff.

01:06:18.920 --> 01:06:22.085
You could have a
Huber penalty, right?

01:06:22.085 --> 01:06:24.900
Where it starts out as--
it starts out as a square

01:06:24.900 --> 01:06:28.220
and then transitions
to linear, right?

01:06:28.220 --> 01:06:30.650
So it looks like an
L1 for large things.

01:06:30.650 --> 01:06:32.540
And then that's
going to play really

01:06:32.540 --> 01:06:35.300
well when there are really big
outliers in your data, right?

01:06:35.300 --> 01:06:36.440
So these-- OK.

01:06:36.440 --> 01:06:40.400
These are things you should
have in your mind by now I hope.

01:06:40.400 --> 01:06:42.260
So it shouldn't
be too surprising

01:06:42.260 --> 01:06:47.816
that the problem of
handling outliers

01:06:47.816 --> 01:06:49.976
is related to this
cardinality thing.

01:06:53.560 --> 01:06:55.062
So here's the measurement model.

01:06:55.062 --> 01:06:56.770
It says I'm going to
measure some things.

01:06:56.770 --> 01:06:58.353
They are linear
functions of the thing

01:06:58.353 --> 01:07:02.110
I want to guess which
is x plus a Gaussian.

01:07:02.110 --> 01:07:02.980
That's fine.

01:07:02.980 --> 01:07:04.920
And then plus wi.

01:07:04.920 --> 01:07:07.780
wi is simply assumed
to be sparse.

01:07:07.780 --> 01:07:08.980
Now what this means?

01:07:08.980 --> 01:07:11.410
Otherwise, that we have no--

01:07:11.410 --> 01:07:13.255
I say nothing about
the size of ai.

01:07:13.255 --> 01:07:15.493
I have no statistical
model for wi, sorry.

01:07:15.493 --> 01:07:16.660
I have no statistical model.

01:07:16.660 --> 01:07:17.160
Nothing.

01:07:17.160 --> 01:07:18.280
I just say it's sparse.

01:07:18.280 --> 01:07:20.260
Oh, and it has only k nonzeros.

01:07:20.260 --> 01:07:23.020
And what this really
means is that k

01:07:23.020 --> 01:07:27.100
up to k of these measurements
are completely wrong.

01:07:27.100 --> 01:07:28.930
Because wi can be anything.

01:07:28.930 --> 01:07:30.520
We didn't say what wi could be.

01:07:30.520 --> 01:07:32.500
So it basically says,
I'm going to give you

01:07:32.500 --> 01:07:33.500
a bunch of measurements.

01:07:33.500 --> 01:07:35.350
I'll give you m measurements.

01:07:35.350 --> 01:07:37.810
I will tell you the
following. k of them

01:07:37.810 --> 01:07:40.900
can be completely wrong.

01:07:40.900 --> 01:07:43.480
And then you'd say
please fit a model.

01:07:43.480 --> 01:07:44.870
Everybody following this?

01:07:44.870 --> 01:07:48.490
So that's a very strong
outlier formulation.

01:07:48.490 --> 01:07:51.700
Handling an outlier-- but it's
a perfectly good one, right?

01:07:51.700 --> 01:07:54.800
And you could write
this lots of ways.

01:07:54.800 --> 01:07:56.590
One way is you could
write it this way.

01:07:56.590 --> 01:07:59.950
It says that you are
going to select--

01:07:59.950 --> 01:08:03.745
you're going to select
the measurements.

01:08:03.745 --> 01:08:05.200
And you will select--

01:08:05.200 --> 01:08:09.250
you're going to select a set
of measurements to ignore.

01:08:09.250 --> 01:08:12.550
So you're going to say thank
you for your n measurements.

01:08:12.550 --> 01:08:13.780
In my opinion, I'm going to--

01:08:13.780 --> 01:08:19.090
I think that the 11th, the
22nd, the 48th, and the 137th,

01:08:19.090 --> 01:08:20.680
I think they're
complete nonsense.

01:08:20.680 --> 01:08:22.450
I don't believe them, right?

01:08:22.450 --> 01:08:24.825
So you take that and
then you would solve--

01:08:24.825 --> 01:08:26.950
you would toss them out
and solve the least squares

01:08:26.950 --> 01:08:27.590
problem.

01:08:27.590 --> 01:08:29.574
And you can rewrite
that immediately

01:08:29.574 --> 01:08:32.170
as just a simple
cardinality constraint.

01:08:32.170 --> 01:08:34.900
It looks like this, right?

01:08:34.900 --> 01:08:36.025
Let's walk through this.

01:08:38.580 --> 01:08:42.250
w, other than this, it's
not constrained at all.

01:08:42.250 --> 01:08:45.794
So if a wi is nonzero, this
wi can be anything you like.

01:08:45.794 --> 01:08:51.060
And I can actually choose that
wi to equal this residual here.

01:08:51.060 --> 01:08:54.510
In which case, that
means y is ax, right?

01:08:54.510 --> 01:08:55.660
So that's fine.

01:08:55.660 --> 01:08:59.170
So if you work out what this is,
it's exactly the same as that.

01:08:59.170 --> 01:09:01.080
That makes sense?

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:05.229
There, so that's the idea there.

01:09:05.229 --> 01:09:05.729
OK.

01:09:08.374 --> 01:09:11.090
Fascinating area
has to do with--

01:09:11.090 --> 01:09:13.340
we talked a little bit about
this when we talked about

01:09:13.340 --> 01:09:16.279
phase one methods is minimum
number of violations.

01:09:16.279 --> 01:09:19.670
So I give you a set of
convex constraints like m

01:09:19.670 --> 01:09:23.479
specifications or something.

01:09:23.479 --> 01:09:25.380
If they're all feasible, great.

01:09:25.380 --> 01:09:27.170
If they're not all
feasible, then I

01:09:27.170 --> 01:09:30.810
might want to say
something like this.

01:09:30.810 --> 01:09:35.430
Please find an x that
violates as few as possible.

01:09:35.430 --> 01:09:37.290
Make sense?

01:09:37.290 --> 01:09:38.609
That would be the picture.

01:09:38.609 --> 01:09:39.899
You could put weights on it.

01:09:39.899 --> 01:09:40.649
It doesn't matter.

01:09:40.649 --> 01:09:44.170
But here, just violate them
as few as few as possible.

01:09:44.170 --> 01:09:47.250
And so that, you could
write down this way.

01:09:47.250 --> 01:09:49.319
I introduce a slack variable t.

01:09:49.319 --> 01:09:52.920
If t is zero, then it
means you've actually

01:09:52.920 --> 01:09:54.550
satisfied the i-th inequality.

01:09:54.550 --> 01:09:59.742
But if t is positive, it means
you might not have, right?

01:09:59.742 --> 01:10:01.950
And, of course, if you're
minimizing the cardinality,

01:10:01.950 --> 01:10:04.710
if you did satisfy it, you'd
take t equals 0 here, right?

01:10:04.710 --> 01:10:09.840
So this does exactly what
you're asked to do, right?

01:10:09.840 --> 01:10:11.290
This makes sense?

01:10:11.290 --> 01:10:12.780
So this would be that--

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:13.920
Yeah.

01:10:13.920 --> 01:10:16.150
And we'll see all sorts
of cool things happen.

01:10:16.150 --> 01:10:16.840
Yes?

01:10:16.840 --> 01:10:19.980
Well, isn't this like the
feasibility thing we did.

01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:21.690
We had like one transpose s--

01:10:21.690 --> 01:10:22.768
Yes.

01:10:22.768 --> 01:10:24.060
We could solve that one, right?

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:24.930
Yes, you can.

01:10:24.930 --> 01:10:25.780
Yeah.

01:10:25.780 --> 01:10:26.280
OK.

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:27.447
You have to be very careful.

01:10:27.447 --> 01:10:30.735
You can quote, "solve," unquote
it if that's what you mean.

01:10:30.735 --> 01:10:32.640
But I guess here the
right way is-- the way

01:10:32.640 --> 01:10:34.440
to say it that's
official would be

01:10:34.440 --> 01:10:38.940
to say we have a powerful
heuristic for approximately

01:10:38.940 --> 01:10:42.810
solving that problem comma
based on convex optimization.

01:10:42.810 --> 01:10:45.260
That would be the
true statement.

01:10:45.260 --> 01:10:47.840
You can't solve this problem.

01:10:47.840 --> 01:10:48.390
OK.

01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:48.890
Right.

01:10:48.890 --> 01:10:52.518
Well, you can if there's
10 inequalities, right?

01:10:52.518 --> 01:10:53.810
But other than that, you can't.

01:10:53.810 --> 01:10:56.990
If there's 100 inequalities,
you can't solve that problem.

01:10:56.990 --> 01:10:58.910
[INAUDIBLE] maybe I'm
just misremembering.

01:10:58.910 --> 01:11:00.680
I thought that's what
we were doing when

01:11:00.680 --> 01:11:02.780
we had like the 1 transpose s--

01:11:02.780 --> 01:11:03.510
Nope.

01:11:03.510 --> 01:11:04.010
Nope.

01:11:04.010 --> 01:11:08.180
The 1 transpose s on the
slack, that was simply

01:11:08.180 --> 01:11:12.200
a heuristic for approximately
solving that problem,

01:11:12.200 --> 01:11:14.360
but you don't
solve that problem.

01:11:14.360 --> 01:11:14.970
OK.

01:11:14.970 --> 01:11:15.470
Yeah.

01:11:15.470 --> 01:11:16.700
I mean, you might
solve it, but you

01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:18.050
won't know you've solved it.

01:11:18.050 --> 01:11:19.860
That's another way
to say it, right?

01:11:19.860 --> 01:11:20.360
Yeah.

01:11:20.360 --> 01:11:26.180
In fact, in many cases,
you probably do, right?

01:11:26.180 --> 01:11:26.680
Yeah.

01:11:26.680 --> 01:11:29.500
So and yes, they're going to
be related to this, right?

01:11:32.945 --> 01:11:33.610
OK.

01:11:33.610 --> 01:11:36.660
So good example is
a linear classifier.

01:11:36.660 --> 01:11:42.170
So I give you a whole bunch
of points with labels, right?

01:11:42.170 --> 01:11:44.270
Say with a Boolean label, yi.

01:11:44.270 --> 01:11:50.280
And you know, what you want is
you want to find w and v. Yeah.

01:11:50.280 --> 01:11:53.888
So this says that you want
this to look like that.

01:11:53.888 --> 01:11:56.430
You want all these to be less
than or equal to zero or bigger

01:11:56.430 --> 01:11:57.055
and equal zero.

01:11:57.055 --> 01:12:00.600
It wouldn't matter whatever
your convention is, right?

01:12:00.600 --> 01:12:02.770
And so then you
can rewrite this.

01:12:02.770 --> 01:12:04.830
If you want the
classifier that makes

01:12:04.830 --> 01:12:07.590
the fewest number
of mistakes, it's

01:12:07.590 --> 01:12:10.380
going to be one of these
convex cardinalities.

01:12:10.380 --> 01:12:14.010
It's going to be
this one, right?

01:12:14.010 --> 01:12:18.660
By the way, when we do
our magic or approximation

01:12:18.660 --> 01:12:21.390
or whatever it is and we
make this into an L1 problem,

01:12:21.390 --> 01:12:24.905
what do you imagine this is
going to turn into roughly?

01:12:28.310 --> 01:12:31.170
It's going to turn into a
support vector machine roughly,

01:12:31.170 --> 01:12:31.670
right?

01:12:31.670 --> 01:12:33.800
So in fact, if I add
L2 regularization,

01:12:33.800 --> 01:12:35.300
that's exactly what it is.

01:12:35.300 --> 01:12:35.885
So OK.

01:12:41.510 --> 01:12:45.750
Here's another weird one,
which is not the same.

01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:47.870
So the story starts
the same way.

01:12:47.870 --> 01:12:50.960
I have a set of
infeasible inequalities.

01:12:50.960 --> 01:12:54.110
In the previous story, which
was a weird positive 1,

01:12:54.110 --> 01:12:58.250
it would basically say
ignore the fewest number

01:12:58.250 --> 01:13:01.370
of inequalities to make that
feasible and you go cool.

01:13:01.370 --> 01:13:04.640
Of your 22 inequalities,
I'm going to--

01:13:04.640 --> 01:13:07.130
if I just toss out these
five, then the rest

01:13:07.130 --> 01:13:08.060
I can make feasible.

01:13:08.060 --> 01:13:09.800
Everybody-- that's
what we're doing?

01:13:09.800 --> 01:13:13.940
This one says to identify
what is the essence

01:13:13.940 --> 01:13:15.650
of the mutual infeasibility.

01:13:15.650 --> 01:13:18.140
So what you're going to do
is take the original 22.

01:13:18.140 --> 01:13:19.490
They're mutually infeasible.

01:13:19.490 --> 01:13:21.800
And I want to get a
subset of, let's say,

01:13:21.800 --> 01:13:23.540
ideal would be like three.

01:13:23.540 --> 01:13:27.800
I could say the
fifth, 10th, and 11th.

01:13:27.800 --> 01:13:30.020
Those three together
are infeasible.

01:13:30.020 --> 01:13:33.830
That means, you can forget
about the other 19, right?

01:13:33.830 --> 01:13:37.520
Because it says that's where--
it's somewhere in these three.

01:13:37.520 --> 01:13:39.680
And then you negotiate
and you go back

01:13:39.680 --> 01:13:43.020
and you find-- you look at
these three inequalities that

01:13:43.020 --> 01:13:46.845
are the bottleneck and you
try to figure out some way

01:13:46.845 --> 01:13:49.710
to either expand or
whatever to go and negotiate

01:13:49.710 --> 01:13:52.320
for a bigger right-hand side
inequality or something.

01:13:52.320 --> 01:13:53.460
Bigger budget.

01:13:53.460 --> 01:13:55.320
OK.

01:13:55.320 --> 01:13:57.430
So that's it.

01:13:57.430 --> 01:14:01.005
So this also is going to
work that way as well.

01:14:01.005 --> 01:14:07.080
So if here, if you work out
the certificate of infeasible--

01:14:07.080 --> 01:14:09.030
of infeasibility,
it basically says

01:14:09.030 --> 01:14:11.460
that if the inf over x--
this is the dual function.

01:14:11.460 --> 01:14:12.960
If that's bigger
than or equal to 1,

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:16.110
actually all you need is to be
positive, but it's homogeneous.

01:14:16.110 --> 01:14:17.820
So that's good enough.

01:14:17.820 --> 01:14:21.030
If lambda is positive and this
[INAUDIBLE] is bigger than 1,

01:14:21.030 --> 01:14:24.420
then this collection
here of inequalities

01:14:24.420 --> 01:14:25.710
is mutually infeasible.

01:14:25.710 --> 01:14:27.270
And so now what
you're going to do

01:14:27.270 --> 01:14:29.470
is you simply minimize
the cardinality.

01:14:29.470 --> 01:14:31.950
So you'd put a comment
next to these things.

01:14:31.950 --> 01:14:33.630
You'd put a comment
here that said

01:14:33.630 --> 01:14:38.580
this is a certificate proving
that these inequalities are

01:14:38.580 --> 01:14:40.395
mutually infeasible.

01:14:40.395 --> 01:14:42.960
And if you put-- if you
minimize the cardinality,

01:14:42.960 --> 01:14:47.520
you will get this-- you
will get the smallest group

01:14:47.520 --> 01:14:52.830
subset of mutually infeasible
inequalities, right?

01:14:52.830 --> 01:14:55.080
So which is actually
very cool, right?

01:14:55.080 --> 01:14:58.110
I mean, could be quite useful.

01:14:58.110 --> 01:15:00.450
OK.

01:15:00.450 --> 01:15:02.115
We'll give one more
example and then

01:15:02.115 --> 01:15:03.990
we'll come back next
time and talk about what

01:15:03.990 --> 01:15:05.400
to do about these, right?

01:15:05.400 --> 01:15:06.720
You also get things in--

01:15:06.720 --> 01:15:09.000
I mean, here's an
example from finance.

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:12.090
That doesn't apply
to big hedge funds,

01:15:12.090 --> 01:15:15.360
but it does apply
to people, right?

01:15:15.360 --> 01:15:19.260
This actually comes up in
actually in crypto trading.

01:15:19.260 --> 01:15:20.790
It's the same story.

01:15:20.790 --> 01:15:23.770
What happens is the following.

01:15:23.770 --> 01:15:25.680
You have a trading
fee, which might

01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:28.080
be proportional, first
of all, to the amount

01:15:28.080 --> 01:15:30.000
that you buy or sell.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:32.130
That's normal.

01:15:32.130 --> 01:15:35.580
But you also have one
that says that is actually

01:15:35.580 --> 01:15:39.510
if you buy or sell at all, I'm
going to charge you $3 or $9

01:15:39.510 --> 01:15:40.875
or something like that.

01:15:40.875 --> 01:15:43.860
And I forget what this is
called in crypto trading,

01:15:43.860 --> 01:15:48.510
but you have to provide
a gas fee or something.

01:15:48.510 --> 01:15:50.010
Anyway something
like that, right?

01:15:50.010 --> 01:15:52.725
And that's what it would be.

01:15:52.725 --> 01:15:55.650
If you propose to trade it
all, you pay a simple fee

01:15:55.650 --> 01:15:57.950
to request the trade.

01:15:57.950 --> 01:15:59.375
In that case,
there would be no--

01:15:59.375 --> 01:16:01.710
this alpha would be 0
or something like that.

01:16:01.710 --> 01:16:02.210
OK.

01:16:02.210 --> 01:16:04.970
And your budget constraint
might look like this.

01:16:04.970 --> 01:16:09.410
That 1 transpose
x is-- so here, x

01:16:09.410 --> 01:16:11.630
is either positive
or negative, right?

01:16:11.630 --> 01:16:12.260
Or here.

01:16:12.260 --> 01:16:14.927
I guess we want to-- we're going
to purchase some dollar amount.

01:16:14.927 --> 01:16:16.925
So it's all positive.

01:16:16.925 --> 01:16:20.750
That's the gross cost of
what you're going to buy.

01:16:20.750 --> 01:16:25.140
This would be the linear-- the
so-called linear cost here.

01:16:25.140 --> 01:16:28.160
And then this part
would be the charge

01:16:28.160 --> 01:16:30.500
or simply, if you
buy 14 things, you're

01:16:30.500 --> 01:16:33.680
going to buy 14 times
whatever some fixed charges.

01:16:33.680 --> 01:16:34.760
OK?

01:16:34.760 --> 01:16:37.505
And then you would end up with
a problem that looks like that,

01:16:37.505 --> 01:16:38.005
right?

01:16:38.005 --> 01:16:43.500
So without this, it's convex,
of course, right, without that.

01:16:43.500 --> 01:16:44.960
So without that, it's convex.

01:16:44.960 --> 01:16:45.930
That's an example.

01:16:45.930 --> 01:16:46.430
OK.

01:16:46.430 --> 01:16:50.150
So I think well, we're going
to quit here for today.

